.TH KARIDNS\-WEBADMIN 8 "2026-11-13" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karidns-webadmin.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karidns\-webadmin \- the service that gives people and programs an HTTP API for
the zones, users and grants of a KariDNS database. This manual is for the person
who installs and runs it: packages, database roles, the first administrator,
TLS, backups, upgrades, and what to watch. The API itself (every endpoint, every
error code, every setting) is in the \fBAPI reference\fR.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
karidns\-webadmin [\-c config] [\-\-check]
karidns\-webadmin [\-c config] \-\-create\-admin NAME [\-\-bootstrap\-conninfo\-file FILE]
karidns\-webadmin \-\-version | \-h | \-\-help
.fi
.SH OVERVIEW
.RS 2
.nf
 browser (item F) / scripts with API tokens
        |  HTTPS            (a reverse proxy: nginx, relayd; or the built\-in TLS)
        v
 karidns\-webadmin           Node.js 22.13+, one process, user karidns\-webadmin, 127.0.0.1:8053 or a UNIX socket
        |  SQL  (PostgreSQL: role karidns_api, row level security on;  SQLite3: the zone file + the auth file)
        |  runs: karidns\-zone (read and write zone data), karicheck, karictl (SQLite3: the reload)
        v
 the database  <\-\-\-\- karidns\-dbloader <\-\-\-\- karidns (the DNS server), and karidns\-signer (optional)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The service decides \fBwho may do what\fR (users and groups, three kinds of
grant, record policies), checks every change set against the zone rules, and
writes it through \fBkaridns\-zone\fR. It does not serve DNS and does not talk
to a DNS server, except to ask \fBkarictl\fR for a reload on SQLite3. If the
database is PostgreSQL, row level security checks every statement a second time
as the database role \fBkaridns_api\fR; on SQLite3 the service's own checks are
the only checks (read "The two models" below before you choose).
.SS The two models
.TS
lbw(22) lbw(23) lbw(23)
lw(22) lw(23) lw(23).
\&	\&PostgreSQL	\&SQLite3
_
T{
Who may read or write what
T}	T{
the service \fBand\fR the database (row level security, the audit triggers)
T}	\&the service only
T{
A bug in the service that allows too much
T}	T{
the database still refuses (and the log says \fBrow level security refused what
the API allowed (a bug)\fR)
T}	\&the change is made
T{
Someone who can write the files
T}	T{
n/a (they are not on the host)
T}	T{
can do anything: the zone file and the auth file are the credential
T}
T{
Several DNS servers on one database
T}	T{
yes (reload requests reach every server)
T}	T{
no: one host (the file is local)
T}
\&Choose it when	T{
several hosts, several teams, tenants that must not be able to harm each other
T}	T{
one host, a small team that trusts the host
T}
.TE
.SH INSTALLING
.SS The packages
.RS 2
.nf
pkg add karidns\-postgresql\-0.6.2\-FreeBSD\-15\-amd64.pkg      # or karidns\-sqlite3, or karidns\-db: they hold karidns, karicheck, karictl, karidns\-zone, the loader and db/*.sql
pkg install node24                                         # pkg add does not fetch dependencies
pkg add karidns\-webadmin\-0.6.2\-FreeBSD\-15\-amd64.pkg
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR installs \fB/usr/local/sbin/karidns\-webadmin\fR (a
wrapper that starts the Node.js of the package with core dumps off),
\fB/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-webadmin/\fR (the program, its one dependency
\fBpg\fR, and the code it shares with \fBkaridns\-signer\fR; root-owned,
read-only), \fB/usr/local/etc/rc.d/karidns_webadmin\fR,
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-webadmin.conf.sample\fR, the web interface
(\fBwebadmin/ui/\fR under the program directory), these manuals in
\fB/usr/local/share/doc/karidns/\fR (\fBkaridns\-webadmin.md\fR,
\fBkaridns\-api.md\fR, \fBkaridns\-webadmin\-gui.md\fR,
\fBkaridns\-webadmin\-gui\-checklist.md\fR), and creates the user and group
\fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR (no login shell, no password, home
\fB/var/db/karidns\-webadmin\fR). The package refuses to install without
\fB/usr/local/bin/karidns\-zone\fR and \fB/usr/local/bin/karicheck\fR (the
database packages conflict with each other, so the dependency cannot name
one). The SQL files are in \fB/usr/local/share/karidns/db/\fR.
.PP
From a source tree (no package): \fBcd webadmin && npm ci
\-\-ignore\-scripts\fR, then \fBnode webadmin/bin/karidns\-webadmin.js \-\-check
\-c <config>\fR. The dependency is pinned by \fBpackage\-lock.json\fR; there are
no install scripts, and CI runs \fBnpm audit \-\-audit\-level=high\fR.
.SS Files the service uses
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-webadmin.conf\fR
\fIOwner, mode:\fR root:karidns-webadmin, 0640
.br
\fIHolds:\fR the settings (no secret)
.TP 8
\fBapi.conninfo\fR, \fBcheck.conninfo\fR (PostgreSQL)
\fIOwner, mode:\fR karidns-webadmin, \fB0600\fR
.br
\fIHolds:\fR the connection of the roles \fBkaridns_api\fR and the check role,
with passwords if the database needs them; \fB\-\-check\fR and the start refuse
any other owner or mode
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-webadmin/webadmin/ui/\fR
\fIOwner, mode:\fR root, 0444 files in 0755 directories
.br
\fIHolds:\fR the files of the web interface (\fBui\-dir\fR); read once at the
start, served under \fB/\fR and \fB/ui/\fR
.TP 8
\fB/var/db/karidns\-webadmin/totp.key\fR
\fIOwner, mode:\fR karidns-webadmin, \fB0600\fR
.br
\fIHolds:\fR the AES-256 key that wraps the TOTP secrets in the database; made
at the first start; the directory is made by the rc script (0700)
.TP 8
the auth file, \fBauth.sqlite3\fR (SQLite3)
\fIOwner, mode:\fR karidns-webadmin, \fB0600\fR
.br
\fIHolds:\fR users, password hashes, grants, sessions, tokens, templates;
\fB\-\-check\fR refuses a wider mode
.TP 8
the zone file, \fBzones.sqlite3\fR (SQLite3)
\fIOwner, mode:\fR karidns-webadmin, group of the loader, 0660, directory
writable by that group
.br
\fIHolds:\fR the zones; SQLite3 opens it read-write even to read (WAL mode)
.TP 8
\fBtls.key\fR (only for the built-in TLS)
\fIOwner, mode:\fR karidns-webadmin, \fB0600\fR
.br
\fIHolds:\fR the certificate key; \fB\-\-check\fR refuses any other owner or
mode
.TP 8
\fBwebadmin\-karictl.conf\fR (SQLite3, optional)
\fIOwner, mode:\fR karidns-webadmin, \fB0600\fR
.br
\fIHolds:\fR the restricted control key for the reload
.TP 8
\fBoidc.secret\fR (one for each \fB[oidc]\fR section, optional)
\fIOwner, mode:\fR karidns-webadmin, \fB0600\fR
.br
\fIHolds:\fR the client secret that the identity provider gave the service; one
line; \fB\-\-check\fR and the start refuse any other owner or mode
.SS PostgreSQL
The owner of the PowerDNS tables does this once (and again at every upgrade: the
files can be applied again):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
psql \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-f /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql karidns                       # schema version 3 and the karidns_auth schema
psql \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-v role=karidns_api   \-v schema=public \-f /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_api_role.pgsql.sql   karidns
psql \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-v role=karidns_check \-v schema=public \-f /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_check_role.pgsql.sql karidns
psql \-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \-v role=karidns_loader \-v schema=public \-f /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql karidns    # if not done already
.fi
.RE
.PP
What this sets up, and why two roles:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkaridns_api\fB\fR is what the service connects as for everything it does
for a user. Row level security (RLS) is switched on by its script: every row it
reads or writes must be covered by a grant of the acting user
(\fBkaridns.principal\fR, set in every transaction). It cannot read the key
material (\fBcryptokeys.content\fR), cannot write the audit log, and has no
\fBBYPASSRLS\fR. The script stops, before it changes anything, if the role is a
superuser, owns the tables, or is a member of \fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR \[u2014]
RLS would not bind it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkaridns_check\fB\fR is the role of the \fIcheck runs\fR: \fBkaridns\-zone
apply \-\-dry\-run\fR has to see the \fBwhole\fR zone to check a change (a CNAME
beside other data, the size limits), and a subdomain master's session sees only
its part. The check role sees every row but \fBcan never commit\fR: a deferred
trigger on every table it writes refuses the COMMIT. A compromised service could
use the check role to \fIread\fR whole zones, not to change them.
.IP \(bu 2
Both scripts create the role as \fBNOLOGIN\fR if it does not exist. Make login
roles that are members of them, \fBnot\fR the roles themselves and not members
of \fBkaridns_rls_trusted\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
CREATE ROLE karidns_web   LOGIN PASSWORD '...' IN ROLE karidns_api;
CREATE ROLE karidns_webck LOGIN PASSWORD '...' IN ROLE karidns_check;
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
# /usr/local/etc/karidns/api.conninfo   (karidns\-webadmin, 0600)
host=db.example.test port=5432 dbname=karidns user=karidns_web password=... sslmode=verify\-full
# /usr/local/etc/karidns/check.conninfo (the same, user=karidns_webck)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a UNIX socket (\fBhost=/tmp\fR) or \fBsslmode=verify\-full\fR for a remote
database. The keywords understood are \fBhost\fR, \fBport\fR, \fBdbname\fR,
\fBuser\fR, \fBpassword\fR, \fBsslmode\fR, \fBconnect_timeout\fR and
\fBapplication_name\fR; any other is an error. \fB\fBsslmode=prefer\fB and
\fBallow\fB do not try TLS\fR (the Node driver has no opportunistic mode): they
mean plain text (so does no \fBsslmode\fR at all). Use \fBrequire\fR (encrypted,
the certificate not checked), \fBverify\-ca\fR or \fBverify\-full\fR;
\fBverify\-full\fR is the one that keeps another host from posing as the
database. \fBsslrootcert\fR is not read: the certificate is checked against
Node's CA list, so a database whose certificate comes from your own CA needs
that CA in the environment of the service
(\fBNODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/path/to/ca.pem\fR). When \fBapi\-conninfo\-file\fR
names a host that is not local (a Unix socket directory, \fBlocalhost\fR,
\fB127.0.0.0/8\fR and \fB::1\fR are local) and the \fBsslmode\fR is not
\fBrequire\fR, \fBverify\-ca\fR or \fBverify\-full\fR, \fB\-\-check\fR prints
\fBwarning: the connection to the database at HOST is not encrypted (sslmode=X);
use sslmode=verify\-full (require encrypts but does not check the server)\fR and
the start writes the same warning to the log. It is a warning, not a refusal.
.PP
Several data schemas (views with their own zone data): run the role scripts once
per schema and list the schemas in \fB[dataset NAME]\fR sections of the
configuration (API reference, "Configuration file").
.SS SQLite3
.RS 2
.nf
sqlite3 /var/db/karidns/zones.sqlite3 < /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql        # schema version 3
sqlite3 /var/db/karidns/zones.sqlite3 < /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_audit.sqlite3.sql      # the audit triggers (without them the audit log stays empty)
install \-m 0600 \-o karidns\-webadmin \-g karidns\-webadmin /dev/null /var/db/karidns/auth.sqlite3
su \-m karidns\-webadmin \-c 'sqlite3 /var/db/karidns/auth.sqlite3 < /usr/local/share/karidns/db/karidns_auth.sqlite3.sql'
chown karidns\-webadmin:<the loader group> /var/db/karidns/zones.sqlite3; chmod 0660 /var/db/karidns/zones.sqlite3
.fi
.RE
.PP
The zone file is in WAL mode; the directory must be writable by the service
\fBand\fR by the loader's user (they both create the \fB\-wal\fR and \fB\-shm\fR
files). The \fBloader must not be able to read the auth file\fR (it holds the
password hashes): that is why it is a file of its own. Everything in the zone
file and the auth file is protected by file permissions alone; read the first
row of "The two models".
.PP
\fBReload with SQLite3.\fR There are no reload requests on SQLite3, so the
service asks the running server with \fBkarictl\fR, using a key that can do
nothing but \fBreload\-zone\fR (a full-power key must never sit in the web
tier):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
# karidns.conf
key "webadmin" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "..."; };          # a secret of your own:  karictl tsig\-keygen webadmin
control\-channel { socket "/var/run/karidns/control.sock"; allow\-group "karidns\-webadmin"; keys { "webadmin" read\-only yes allow\-commands { reload\-zone; }; }; };
# /usr/local/etc/karidns/webadmin\-karictl.conf   (karidns\-webadmin, 0600); karictl\-config in karidns\-webadmin.conf points here
socket "/var/run/karidns/control.sock";
key "karictl" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "..."; };           # the same secret
.fi
.RE
.PP
The control socket must be reachable by the service user, so on SQLite3 the
service runs on the DNS host. Without \fBkarictl\-config\fR the API changes
zones but cannot reload them; an operator reloads with \fBkarictl\fR.
.SS The configuration
Copy the sample and edit it. A minimal PostgreSQL deployment behind nginx:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
listen = 127.0.0.1:8053
public\-origin = https://dns.example.test
trusted\-proxy = 127.0.0.1
database = pg
api\-conninfo\-file = /usr/local/etc/karidns/api.conninfo
check\-conninfo\-file = /usr/local/etc/karidns/check.conninfo
log = daemon
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
chown root:karidns\-webadmin /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-webadmin.conf; chmod 0640 /usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-webadmin.conf
chown karidns\-webadmin /usr/local/etc/karidns/api.conninfo /usr/local/etc/karidns/check.conninfo; chmod 0600 /usr/local/etc/karidns/*.conninfo
service karidns_webadmin oneconfigtest       # karidns\-webadmin \-\-check as the service user: files and modes, tools, the database schema (the "one" prefix: it works before the service is enabled)
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBThe files of the web interface.\fR The service also serves the files of its
web interface from memory: it reads the directory \fBwebadmin/ui/\fR of the
installation (or the directory \fBui\-dir\fR names) \fBonce, when it starts\fR,
and answers \fB/\fR (the page) and \fB/ui/...\fR (the rest) from what it read;
there is no file access at request time, so a change of the files needs a
restart. Names are letters, digits, \fB.\fR, \fB_\fR and \fB\-\fR, only regular
files, only the types the interface uses (\fBhtml css js mjs json svg png ico
txt\fR), at most 512 files and 8 MiB, and an \fBindex.html\fR; anything else
keeps the service from starting (\fBconfigtest\fR says which file). \fBui =
no\fR switches the interface off (\fB/\fR is then a 404 and only \fB/api/v1/\fR
is served), for an operator who serves the files from elsewhere. The files carry
their own security headers (no inline script, no outside origin, no framing; API
reference, "THE WEB INTERFACE"), so a proxy must not add scripts or rewrite
them.
.PP
\fBconfigtest\fR says \fBok:\fR for what is fine and \fBproblem:\fR for what is
not, and exits 1 on a problem. It checks the files and their modes, that
\fBkaridns\-zone\fR has the driver of the database and speaks the JSON schema
this version expects (and has the \fBrender\fR command of the web interface),
that the files of the web interface can be read, that \fBkaricheck\fR runs, and
that the database is at schema version 3 with every table the service uses.
.SS The first administrator
There is no default account. As the service user, once:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
# SQLite3
su \-m karidns\-webadmin \-c 'karidns\-webadmin \-\-create\-admin admin'
\&
# PostgreSQL: the API role cannot make the first user (row level security), so \-\-create\-admin needs the connection of the table owner.
# Put it in a file of the service user for the minutes this takes, and delete it afterwards.
install \-m 0600 \-o karidns\-webadmin /dev/null /var/db/karidns\-webadmin/owner.conninfo      # then write: host=... dbname=karidns user=<the owner> password=...
su \-m karidns\-webadmin \-c 'karidns\-webadmin \-\-create\-admin admin \-\-bootstrap\-conninfo\-file /var/db/karidns\-webadmin/owner.conninfo'
rm \-P /var/db/karidns\-webadmin/owner.conninfo
.fi
.RE
.PP
It makes the user \fBadmin\fR with a global master grant, the record policy
\fBall\-types\fR, and a random password that is \fBshown once\fR. A second
\fB\-\-create\-admin\fR with the same name is refused; use another name to make
another administrator (this is also the way back in when every administrator is
locked out). Then:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
sysrc karidns_webadmin_enable=YES
service karidns_webadmin start
.fi
.RE
.SS TOTP: the first login
With the default \fBrequire\-totp = global\fR, a global master has to use a
second factor. The first login gets a session that can do nothing but enrol:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
O=https://dns.example.test
curl \-s \-c jar \-H 'Content\-Type: application/json' \-d '{"user":"admin","password":"<the password>"}' $O/api/v1/auth/login
#  \-> {"csrf":"<token>","totp":{"enabled":false,"enrollment_required":true},...}   the session cookie is in the jar
curl \-s \-b jar \-c jar \-H 'Content\-Type: application/json' \-H "Origin: $O" \-H 'X\-KariDNS\-CSRF: <token>' \-d '{"action":"enroll"}' $O/api/v1/auth/totp
#  \-> {"secret":"JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP...","uri":"otpauth://totp/KariDNS:admin?..."}      put the secret in an authenticator app (SHA\-1, 6 digits, 30 s)
curl \-s \-b jar \-c jar \-H 'Content\-Type: application/json' \-H "Origin: $O" \-H 'X\-KariDNS\-CSRF: <token>' \-d '{"action":"confirm","code":"123456"}' $O/api/v1/auth/totp
#  \-> a new session (the jar is updated) and a new csrf token: from now on the login needs "totp"
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBKeep the clock of the server right (\fBntpd\fB).\fR A TOTP code is accepted
for the 30-second step it belongs to and one step before or after, so a server
clock more than about 30 seconds off refuses every correct code, at the login
and at the confirm step above (a trial with a server clock 118 s fast: every
code failed). The same holds for sign-in with an identity provider (below).
.PP
An unsafe request (POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) with a session cookie needs the
\fBX\-KariDNS\-CSRF\fR header \fBand\fR an \fBOrigin\fR equal to
\fBpublic\-origin\fR, and \fBContent\-Type: application/json\fR. Automation
should not use the cookie: make an \fBAPI token\fR (\fBPOST /api/v1/tokens\fR, a
login session is needed to make it) and send \fBAuthorization: Bearer
kdt_...\fR; a token needs no CSRF header, can be read-only, and expires (90 days
by default, 365 at most).
.PP
Then make the real users and grants (API reference, "Permissions"): groups for
the tenants, users in them, record policies (\fBPOST /api/v1/rr\-policies\fR),
grants (\fBPOST /api/v1/grants\fR: \fBdomain_master\fR on a zone,
\fBsubdomain_master\fR on a name below it). A domain master can grant on its own
zone, with a policy no wider than its own, without a global master.
.SH TLS AND THE REVERSE PROXY
The service listens on \fB127.0.0.1\fR or a \fBUNIX socket\fR and speaks plain
HTTP there; it refuses to start with plain HTTP on any other address. TLS is the
job of a reverse proxy in front. \fBpublic\-origin\fR must be the https origin a
browser uses (an unsafe request with a cookie must carry exactly this
\fBOrigin\fR, and the cookie gets the \fBSecure\fR flag and HSTS from it).
.PP
\fBSet \fBtrusted\-proxy\fB\fR to the proxy's address (\fBlocal\fR for a UNIX
socket): the rate limits and the log use the address the proxy reports in
\fBX\-Forwarded\-For\fR (the first from the right that is not a trusted proxy),
and only from a trusted proxy is the header believed. Without it every client
has the proxy's address and shares one login limit.
.SS nginx
.RS 2
.nf
# in the http { } block: a flood of requests with bad credentials costs the service a database lookup each; stop it here
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=webadmin:10m rate=20r/s;
\&
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name dns.example.test;
    ssl_certificate     /usr/local/etc/ssl/dns.example.test.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /usr/local/etc/ssl/dns.example.test.key;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
\&
    client_max_body_size 17m;               # a zone import is up to 16 MiB; everything else is under 1 MiB
    client_header_buffer_size 16k;          # the service refuses more than 16 KiB of headers
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_read_timeout 330s;                # an import may take 300 s
\&
    location / {                            # the API (/api/v1/) and the files of the web interface (/ and /ui/): the service decides what exists, the rest is a 404
        limit_req zone=webadmin burst=40 nodelay;
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8053;                                     # or http://unix:/var/run/karidns_webadmin/api.sock;
        proxy_set_header X\-Forwarded\-For $remote_addr;                       # set, not append: the client's own header is not passed on
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
With a UNIX socket put \fBlisten = /var/run/karidns_webadmin/api.sock\fR and
\fBtrusted\-proxy = local\fR in the configuration (the rc script makes
\fB/var/run/karidns_webadmin\fR for the pid files and the service makes the
socket mode 0660 in it; the proxy's user must be in the group
\fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR to open it). Any other path must be in a directory the
service user can write.
.SS relayd
.RS 2
.nf
table <webadmin> { 127.0.0.1 }
http protocol "webadmin" {
    tls { no tlsv1.0, no tlsv1.1 }
    match request header set "X\-Forwarded\-For" value "$REMOTE_ADDR"          # set: a header from the client is replaced
    block                                                                     # relayd takes the last rule that matches: block everything ...
    pass request path "/api/*"                                                # ... except the API,
    pass request path "/ui/*"                                                 # the files of the web interface
    pass request path "/"                                                     # and its page
}
relay "webadmin_tls" {
    listen on 192.0.2.10 port 443 tls
    protocol "webadmin"
    forward to <webadmin> port 8053
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
(Neither proxy configuration is run by KariDNS's tests; check the syntax against
your version. The relayd example could not be run when this was written: the
package \fBrelayd\fR of FreeBSD 15.1 does not start on a 15.0 userland, so it is
untested even for syntax. \fBrelayd \-n \-f /usr/local/etc/relayd.conf\fR checks
it; the TLS relay also needs its certificate and key (see \fBtls keypair\fR in
relayd.conf(5) for where it looks for them). What the service needs from the
proxy is only: TLS, the paths \fB/api/\fR, \fB/ui/\fR and \fB/\fR forwarded
(\fB/\fR and \fB/ui/\fR only with the web interface, unless \fBui = no\fR),
\fBX\-Forwarded\-For\fR set to the client's address, bodies up to 16 MiB, and a
read timeout longer than 300 seconds for imports.)
.SS The built-in TLS
For a single host without a proxy: set \fBtls\-cert\fR and \fBtls\-key\fR (both,
PEM files; \fBtls\-key\fR owned by the service user, mode 0600, which
\fB\-\-check\fR verifies) and \fBlisten = 192.0.2.10:8443\fR. TLS 1.2 or newer;
the key and certificate are read at the start and again at SIGHUP (\fBservice
karidns_webadmin reload\fR): a renewed certificate needs no restart, new
connections use it, open ones keep the old. There is no HTTP to HTTPS redirect
and no OCSP stapling; a proxy does those better.
.SH THE WEB INTERFACE
The service serves a browser front end of its own API under \fB/\fR (settings
\fBui\fR and \fBui\-dir\fR, API reference, "The web interface"). \fBWhat the
screens are and how to use them \[u2014] by role, with the messages and a
glossary in English and Japanese \[u2014] is the user manual\fR; this section is
for the operator. It is plain JavaScript modules and one style sheet, no build
step, no framework, no outside address: the page's policy
(\fBdefault\-src 'none'\fR, scripts and styles only from the origin, no inline
code, Trusted Types) makes anything else fail, which is why \fBa proxy that
injects a script or a style sheet breaks it\fR (the page then shows a banner
that something was blocked). It needs no database and no login for its files;
everything it shows comes from \fB/api/v1/\fR with the cookie session of the
person (\fBHttpOnly\fR, \fBSameSite=Strict\fR, \fBPath=/api/\fR, so the files
are fetched without it).
.PP
This release has the foundation and the person's own pages: \fBsign-in\fR (user,
password, the code when the account has TOTP; one message for every refusal; a
button that waits when the service says 429), \fBTOTP set-up\fR (the QR code is
drawn in the page by the interface's own encoder, the key as text only when
asked for; a global master under \fBrequire\-totp = global\fR can do nothing
else until it has done this), the \fBaccount\fR (password, TOTP on and off where
the configuration allows, API tokens shown once, language, signing out here or
everywhere), the start page with the person's grants, and \fBAbout\fR
(versions). English and Japanese, chosen by the browser and changed in the
account.
.PP
It also has \fBthe zone screens\fR (item F, milestone M3). Everything they show
is what the API answers; what is hidden is a hint, and every call can still be
refused (the page handles 401, 403, 404, 409, 422, 429 and 503 everywhere):
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZones\fR (menu "Zones"): the zones the person has a grant on, with its role
on each, the kind, the serial and notes (kept signed by the signer, signed
elsewhere, catalog); a dataset selector when there is more than one, a search by
a part of the name or its start, by kind and by signed or not, paged by cursor
("next page", no totals). A global master also has "New zone" and "Update every
zone of this dataset" (PostgreSQL) here.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA zone\fR (sections Records, Import, Settings, DNSSEC, and the export link):
the header shows the kind, the serial and generation, when it changed, the
signer's state ("waiting for the signer"), the last reload request, and the
person's role and record policy. The \fBrecords\fR are read a page at a time
with their text form (\fB?rdata=1\fR, the form a change set takes: names are
absolute, MX and SRV have their numbers in front), names are shown relative to
the zone, a search by a part of the owner name or from a name, and the page
size. A record set the person may not edit is shown with the reason: the zone is
not a primary zone, is signed elsewhere (\fBPRESIGNED\fR without the signer),
has location / ECS tags or tinydns attributes (\fBtagged\fR), is a DNSSEC type,
a record has no text form or is switched off, the type is not in the person's
policy, or the name is outside a subdomain master's suffix or at the apex. The
SOA is shown and is only edited by a global master, in a form of its own that
says the service writes the next serial whatever is typed, and that there is no
precondition on it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBEditing is a draft.\fR Edit, Add and Delete put an entry into a change set
that lives in the memory of the page (nothing is stored in the browser: closing
the tab loses it, and the page asks before it is left while a draft exists; it
survives a new sign-in of the same person after a 401 and is dropped for another
person or at logout). Each entry has an undo. \fBPreview\fR is a dry run: the
diff (before and after per record set, as the service computes it), the messages
of the zone checks with their codes (a message that names a record is shown at
that record), and the version and serial it would write. \fBCommit\fR sends the
same change set with a precondition (\fBexpect\fR: the records as they were
loaded, from the text form) and, if ticked, the reload. The button waits for a
new preview after any change, and waits \fBRetry\-After\fR after a 429 or 503. A
diff of more than 200 record sets is shown in pages of 200.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBConflicts.\fR If somebody changed a record set after it was loaded, the
service answers 409 \fBprecondition\-failed\fR with the record set as it is: the
page shows, per record set, what was loaded, what is there now and the person's
change, and the person takes theirs (the entry is dropped and the page read
again) or keeps their own (the precondition becomes the present state; preview
again). 409 \fBchanged\fR (the zone kept changing), 403 (the record set that no
grant allows is named at its entry) and 422 (the zone checks) are shown where
they belong.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe update button.\fR Changes are in the database after a commit; the servers
serve them after a reload, which is explicit (\fBreload\-after\-change\fR sets
the default of the box, the button "Update the servers now" asks for one). A
zone the signer keeps has no button: the signer asks for the reload when it has
signed (the page says so). After a request the page looks at the zone and at
\fB/servers\fR every 5 seconds for at most two minutes and says how many servers
have loaded the change (PostgreSQL; with SQLite3 there is no server status and
the page asks nothing more). This is the only polling the page does; it extends
the session like any request. The \fBServers\fR screen shows the same per
dataset and server (generation, time, whether every zone loaded, the zones that
did not) and, on SQLite3, the service's note.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNew zone\fR (global masters): from a form (SOA values, name servers, more
records written as \fBname [ttl] TYPE data\fR), from a template (its values are
the defaults of the form) or from a zone file (typed, pasted or read from a
file; the file is decoded as strict UTF-8 and refused otherwise, with the hint
to write other bytes as \fB\eDDD\fR; the size is checked against \fBGET
/version\fR before it is sent). The dry run shows the whole new zone; the commit
goes to its page. A refusal is shown at the field its pointer names.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBImport and export.\fR The import takes a zone file the same way (mode replace
or merge, a dry run first, the diff in pages) and is for the masters of the
whole zone. The export is a plain link to \fB.../export\fR (the service answers
with \fBContent\-Disposition: attachment\fR; the cookie goes with it because it
is the page's own address).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSettings.\fR The attributes (kind, catalog, views, masters), the metadata the
zone has (the kinds the API writes are editable by global masters: one value a
line, a refusal is shown at the box), the catalog and views of the zone, and
deleting the zone, which asks for its name to be typed again (the API's
\fBconfirm\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDNSSEC\fR (domain and global masters): the signer's state (when it signed,
the serial, when it is due, the time left until the first signature expires, the
last error as text), the keys with their key tags, the DS records for the parent
zone with copy buttons (digest types 2 and 4, computed by the service), the key
requests with their state, and a form for \fBenable\fR (not signed yet) or
\fBadd\fR (kept by the signer). Keys and signatures are the signer's: this only
asks.
.PP
The history, the templates, the administration and the audit log are the screens
of item F, milestone M4:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBVersions\fR (section "Versions" of a zone; every person with a grant on the
zone): every saved change is a version, listed newest first with its number,
kind (created, edit, template applied, import, rollback), time, who (the person,
or \fBoperator (<database role>)\fR for a tool such as \fBkaridns\-zone
import\fR), the comment, the serial before and after and the number of record
sets it changed. A zone keeps its newest 100 versions, so the list is one page;
the audit log is the complete record. For the masters of the whole zone the list
marks \fBchanges outside the versions\fR (a write that did not go through
\fBkaridns\-zone\fR: \fBpsql\fR, a PowerDNS tool): between two versions, or
since the newest one, with the record sets the audit log names, or, when the
audit log has nothing for that time, only that the zone's generation grew. A
subdomain master sees the part of each change under its suffix, and no marker. A
number opens \fBone version\fR: its fields and the change it made, as the diff
the editor shows; \fBa difference\fR (the form of the list, or "Against the zone
now" of a row) compares version A with a later version or with the zone as it is
(\fB\-\fR lines are the record set after version A, \fB+\fR lines the other end;
a version that was too big to keep its record sets, or a range of more than
50,000 record sets, is refused by the service and told).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRollback\fR (on the page of a version): the dry run first (the diff, how many
record sets would be restored and how many versions undone, the SOA that is not
restored because its serial only grows), then the commit, with the comment and
the reload box like any change. A record set that something outside the versions
changed after the versions that would be undone is a \fBconflict\fR: the commit
stays off, a table lists each (what the newest version left, what is there now)
and the way on is a second question, "Roll back anyway and overwrite these",
that names the number of record sets it overwrites; that one commit carries
\fBforce\fR. A conflict that appears only at the commit (409
\fBversion\-conflict\fR) is shown the same way. One grant must allow every
record set restored (403 otherwise, at the record set). \fBkarictl rollback\fR
(the server's memory, for emergencies) and this rollback (the database, durable,
re-signed for signed zones) are told apart in the API reference.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTemplates\fR (menu "Templates"; everybody reads, global masters write): the
list with a search by name, a template with its SOA defaults and its records
(owner relative to the zone, \fB@\fR the apex, type, TTL, data), "Check again"
(the zone checks, \fBkaricheck\fR, as of now), and for global masters the form:
name, description, the six SOA fields, and the records as text, one a line,
\fBowner [TTL] TYPE data\fR (lines that begin with \fB;\fR are
ignored). \fBCheck\fR is a dry run; a refusal of the zone checks (422) is shown
with the line of each record in the box. Deleting asks first; zones made from a
template keep their records. In the section "Template" of a zone (the masters of
the whole zone) a template is \fBapplied\fR: its record sets replace the zone's
record sets of the same name and type, with a preview (what would be
overwritten) and the same commit as any change; to undo it, roll back to the
version before.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAdministration\fR (menu "Administration", global masters; a domain master and
a subdomain master get a notice and the page makes no request): \fIUsers\fR and
\fIGroups\fR (search by name, a new user with a password, a new group), the page
of one user or group (name, display name, e-mail, disabled; a disabled user is
signed out and loses its tokens), for a user the state of its sign-in (password
set, two-step verification, failed attempts, locked) with the administrator's
actions: \fBunlock\fR (clears the failed attempts and the lock) and \fBreset
two-step verification\fR (for a user who lost the device: it is switched off and
the user's sessions end; where \fBrequire\-totp\fR says so, the next sign-in
enrols again), each after a question, and a new password (the user's sessions
end and its API tokens are revoked); the groups a user is in; for a group its
members (add by exact name, remove after a question; groups nest). One cannot
disable, delete or reset the two-step verification of oneself (the page does not
offer it; the service answers 409 \fBself\fR). \fIRecord policies\fR (the types,
wildcard, delegation, DNAME and apex flags, the TTL range, the most records; a
policy in use is not deleted: 409). \fIGrants\fR (all grants with a filter by
user or group and by zone; a new grant by role, zone, suffix and policy;
removing asks first) and the same on the page of a principal.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGrants of a zone\fR (section "Grants" of a zone, for its domain master and
for global masters): who holds a grant on this zone and the form to give one. A
domain master may give domain-master and subdomain-master grants on \fBits\fR
zone with a policy no wider than its own; the form offers the policies that look
so (types a subset, each flag not more, TTL range inside, \fBmax_rr\fR not more)
and the service decides (403 \fBescalation\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAudit log\fR (menu "Audit log", global masters and domain masters; a domain
master reads the rows of its zones): newest first, 100 a page ("next page"),
with filters for the zone, the user or group (by name), the change set, the
table, and a time range (\fBsince\fR, \fBuntil\fR, ISO 8601, for example
\fB2026\-11\-08T12:00:00Z\fR). A row shows when, who (the person, or the
database role of a tool), the table and what (added, changed, deleted), the
zone, the change set and the row; "all rows" lists the rows of the same change,
"version" opens the versions of the zone with that version marked (where the
dataset is known). The database rows \fBbefore\fR and \fBafter\fR are shown as
pretty-printed JSON \fBtext\fR (cut at 20,000 characters), never interpreted:
what is in them was written by others.
.PP
All record data, names, comments and error texts are drawn as text: C0 and C1
characters as \fB\eDDD\fR, bidi and zero-width characters as \fB<U+XXXX>\fR, and
never as HTML or as an attribute that decides an address (the tests import
hostile data and check that nothing runs).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSessions.\fR Nothing in the page asks the service on its own, so the idle
time of \fBsession\-idle\fR means what it says: the page counts down from its
last request, warns two minutes before (a minute earlier than the service ends
it, which writes the time of the last request at most once a minute), and "Stay
signed in" is one request made because the person asked. When the session has
ended, a 401 on any page opens the sign-in over what is on the screen; the same
person goes on, another person starts at the start page. A sign-out in one tab
signs out the others (a \fBBroadcastChannel\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBBrowsers.\fR Firefox ESR, Chrome and Edge (current and previous), Safari 17
or newer; no polyfills. The accessibility target is WCAG 2.1 level AA (not
certified); the checklist says how each point is met and has the manual check
for Chrome, Edge and Safari that the project runs before a release. The
automated tests run Firefox (\fBtests/run_webadmin_gui_test.sh\fR on SQLite3 and
\fBtests/run_webadmin_gui_pg_test.sh\fR on PostgreSQL, headless through
geckodriver; the screens of zones need root and a real \fBkaridns\fR, so that
the update button is checked with \fBdag\fR; on the FreeBSD build host that
needs \fBpkg install firefox geckodriver dejavu\fR: Firefox crashes at the start
on a system without a font).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIdentity providers\fR (item F, milestone M5; set-up under SIGNING IN WITH AN
IDENTITY PROVIDER). With \fB[oidc]\fR sections the sign-in screen has a button
for each provider below the password form ("Sign in with Corp ID"), and the
sign-in dialog that opens after a 401 has them too (a click leaves the page; the
browser asks first if there is a draft). The button is a plain link to the
service, which sends the browser to the provider and back to the page the person
was at; if it did not work, the screen says so with one message in the person's
language. A person whose TOTP is on and who comes in through a provider is asked
for the code on a page of its own; a person with no password sees no password
form in the account and gives the code alone to switch TOTP off. The account
shows the identity the user is linked to (read only: a global master links and
unlinks, in the user's page under \fIAdministration\fR). A group has no
identity.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBStorage.\fR The page keeps the chosen language in \fBlocalStorage\fR and
nothing else: no token, no zone data, no secret.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFiles.\fR The interface is in the package (\fBwebadmin/ui/\fR); the page asks
for the version of the service after the sign-in and tells the person to reload
when it differs from its own (the service was upgraded while the page was open).
.SH SIGNING IN WITH AN IDENTITY PROVIDER
People can sign in with an OpenID Connect identity provider (Keycloak, an
enterprise IdP, ...) instead of, or besides, a password. The service is a
confidential client of the authorization code flow with PKCE. It makes no
account for a person who signs in: a \fBglobal master links\fR a user of the
service to the person's identity at the provider, and a sign-in whose identity
is linked to nobody is refused. Setting it up takes four steps; the examples use
the invented provider \fBidp.example.test\fR.
.PP
\fB1. Register the service at the provider.\fR Make a client of the type
"confidential" with the authorization code flow and PKCE (S256), and give it
this redirect URI, exactly (the provider compares it as text):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
https://dns.example.test/api/v1/auth/oidc/corp/callback
.fi
.RE
.PP
that is \fBpublic\-origin\fR, \fB/api/v1/auth/oidc/\fR, the name you give the
provider in the next step, and \fB/callback\fR. Note the client id and the
client secret. The provider must sign ID tokens with RS256, ES256 or EdDSA (the
usual default is RS256) and publish a discovery document at
\fB<issuer>/.well\-known/openid\-configuration\fR.
.PP
\fB2. Put the secret in a file and the provider in the configuration.\fR
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
install \-o karidns\-webadmin \-g karidns\-webadmin \-m 0600 /dev/null /usr/local/etc/karidns/oidc\-corp.secret
printf '%s\en' 'the\-client\-secret' > /usr/local/etc/karidns/oidc\-corp.secret     # one line
.fi
.RE
.PP
and at the end of \fBkaridns\-webadmin.conf\fR (after the global settings):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
[oidc corp]
issuer = https://idp.example.test/realms/corp
client\-id = karidns\-webadmin
client\-secret\-file = /usr/local/etc/karidns/oidc\-corp.secret
label = Corp ID
# scopes = openid                (default; "openid" is always asked for)
# mfa = totp                     (default; see below)
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBissuer\fR is the issuer identifier \fBexactly as the provider writes it\fR in
its discovery document and in its ID tokens (a trailing slash counts: some
providers have one). \fBlabel\fR is the text of the button on the sign-in screen
("Sign in with Corp ID"). Up to 8 providers; each name is 1 to 32 characters
(lower case letters, digits, \fB\-\fR, \fB_\fR) and each issuer once. \fBservice
karidns_webadmin oneconfigtest\fR checks that the secret file is the service
user's and has mode 0600 or 0400, and names the redirect URI of each provider;
\fBit does not contact the provider\fR. Restart the service (or send SIGHUP: the
sections and the secret files are read again, and a mistake in them keeps the
old ones). The service fetches the discovery document and the key set when it
starts and says so in the log (\fBoidc: provider corp (...) is reachable\fR, or
\fB... cannot be used yet: <why>\fR); a provider that is down at that moment
does not stop the service, and is asked again at the next sign-in, at most every
ten seconds.
.PP
\fB3. Allow the egress.\fR The service now makes outbound HTTPS requests: to the
host of the issuer (the discovery document), and to the hosts the discovery
document names for the key set (\fBjwks_uri\fR) and the token endpoint
(\fBtoken_endpoint\fR), which are often, but not always, the same host. If the
machine's firewall (\fBpf\fR) restricts outgoing connections, allow
\fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR to reach those. Each request has 10 seconds and a size
limit, follows no redirect, and refuses an address that is not https, has
credentials, or is an IP address. The proxy in front of the service needs
nothing new: the browser, not the service, talks to the provider's authorization
endpoint, and it comes back to \fBpublic\-origin\fR like any visitor.
.PP
\fB4. Link the users.\fR In the web interface: \fIAdministration\fR, the user,
\fIIdentity at a provider\fR: the provider, the \fBsubject\fR, \fILink the
identity\fR. The subject is the \fBsub\fR claim of the person's ID token, the
provider's own identifier for them (many providers show it in the user's page;
it is \fBnot\fR the e-mail address or the login name, which can change hands:
the service never matches on those). The same through the API:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
curl \-b jar \-H "X\-KariDNS\-CSRF: $CSRF" \-H "Origin: https://dns.example.test" \-H 'Content\-Type: application/json' \e
     \-X PUT \-d '{"issuer":"https://idp.example.test/realms/corp","subject":"8f14e45f\-ceea\-4672\-a1b0\-0c5f4a1f6c11"}' \e
     https://dns.example.test/api/v1/principals/12/oidc
.fi
.RE
.PP
If you do not know the subject yet, have the person press the button: the
sign-in is refused, and the log says which subject it came with (\fBauth: oidc
corp: sign\-in refused from 192.0.2.7: no user is linked to the subject
8f14e45f\-...\fR); link that. A user may be made without a password (\fBPOST
/principals\fR without \fBpassword\fR): such a user signs in with the provider
only. Unlinking an identity (or linking another one) signs the user out;
\fBdisable the user\fR to lock a person out for good (their sessions end and the
sign-in is refused).
.PP
\fBThe second step.\fR \fBrequire\-totp\fR applies to a sign-in with a provider
as it does to a password: where it makes TOTP mandatory for a user (every global
master with the default \fBglobal\fR), or where the user has TOTP on, the
session made by the sign-in can do nothing but the second step: the user sets
TOTP up the first time, and gives the code of the authenticator app afterwards
(the redirect from the provider has no room for a code field, so the page asks
after it). If the provider itself requires two factors and says so in the ID
token (\fBamr\fR, RFC 8176), set \fBmfa = idp\fR in the section: then the step
is left out for sign-ins whose token shows two factors (\fBmfa\fR, or two of
password/PIN, OTP/hardware key/SMS/smart card, fingerprint/face/iris), and only
for them. Do that only if you trust the provider's policy as you trust your own:
it is then the only second factor.
.PP
\fBWhat a failed sign-in looks like.\fR The person returns to the sign-in screen
with one message ("The sign-in with the identity provider was not accepted"),
whatever the reason: a subject that is linked to nobody, a disabled or locked
user, an ID token with a bad signature, a wrong audience or nonce, a state that
is used already or too old, a callback address that was handed to another
browser. The reason is in the log (\fBauth: oidc corp: sign\-in refused from
<address>: <reason>\fR), without secrets; read it to answer "why can't Taro sign
in?". The message "cannot be reached" means the provider or the database did not
answer; "too many attempts" means the rate limit of logins (\fBrate\-login\fR, a
minute, per address; the start and the callback each count) was hit.
.PP
\fBOperating notes.\fR
.IP \(bu 2
A provider that rotates its signing keys needs nothing from you: a token with a
\fBkid\fR the service does not know makes it fetch the key set again (at most
once a minute).
.IP \(bu 2
A provider that changes its issuer or a client secret that is renewed: edit the
section and send SIGHUP. Identities are linked to the \fBissuer string\fR; if
the issuer changes, link the users again.
.IP \(bu 2
Clocks: the service accepts an ID token a minute either side of its time
(\fBexp\fR, \fBiat\fR). Keep the clock of the machine right (\fBntpd\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
The unfinished sign-ins are in \fBkaridns_auth.oidc_states\fR (PostgreSQL) or
the table \fBoidc_states\fR of the auth file (SQLite3): ten minutes each, at
most 1000, deleted when used and swept every hour. The table is not audited and
holds no secret that the provider's answer could use (the PKCE verifier is
useless without the provider's code and the client secret).
.IP \(bu 2
On PostgreSQL the API role has no right on that table:
\fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR (schema version 3) makes it and the three
functions the role calls; \fBrun it again\fR (and
\fBdb/karidns_api_role.pgsql.sql\fR, which grants \fBEXECUTE\fR) when you
upgrade from a build made before the sign-in with a provider. On SQLite3 run
\fBdb/karidns_auth.sqlite3.sql\fR again; \fB\-\-check\fR names a missing table.
.SH RUNNING IT
.RS 2
.nf
service karidns_webadmin start | stop | restart | status
service karidns_webadmin configtest      # karidns\-webadmin \-\-check as the service user
service karidns_webadmin reload          # SIGHUP: reads the configuration again
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBrc.conf\fR variables: \fBkaridns_webadmin_enable\fR,
\fBkaridns_webadmin_config\fR (default
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karidns\-webadmin.conf\fR),
\fBkaridns_webadmin_user\fR (default \fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR; rc.subr runs
\fBdaemon(8)\fR as this user with \fBsu \-m\fR),
\fBkaridns_webadmin_flags\fR. The script makes the directory of the TOTP key and
\fB/var/run/karidns_webadmin\fR for the service user, sets \fBulimit \-c 0\fR,
and starts the program under \fBdaemon \-r\fR, which starts it again 10 seconds
after it dies (for example because the database was down at boot). \fBStandard
error goes to /dev/null under the rc script: set \fBlog = daemon\fB (or another
syslog facility) in the configuration file.\fR If the database is on this host,
start it first (add its service name to \fBREQUIRE\fR in the rc script or to the
\fBrcorder\fR of your site).
.PP
SIGHUP changes the rate limits, the session times, \fBrequire\-totp\fR,
\fBpublic\-origin\fR, \fBtrusted\-proxy\fR, \fBscrypt\-ln\fR and
\fBaudit\-retention\fR, and reads the TLS certificate and key again (the
built-in TLS only); the database, the datasets, \fBworkers\fR, the paths of the
tools, the listen address and switching TLS on or off need a restart. A
configuration with a mistake is refused and the old settings stay (the log says
why).
.SS The log
One line per request, to syslog (\fBlogger(1)\fR, tag \fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR,
\fBnotice\fR): \fB<client address> <request id> <user or \-> <METHOD> <route
template> <status> <ms>ms [cs=<change set>]\fR. Never the query string, a body,
a password, a cookie, a token, or a TOTP code; the user name of an unknown login
is not logged either (what was typed there may be a password). The request id is
also in the \fBX\-Request\-Id\fR header and in every error document: a user who
reports a 500 gives you the id and the log has the rest.
.TP 8
\fBauth: <user> logged in from <address>\fR
a login
.TP 8
\fBauth: login refused for <user> from <address>\fR
a wrong password, a locked account, a user without a password; for a name that
does not exist: \fBan unknown or disabled user\fR
.TP 8
\fBauth: <user> is locked from <network> after N failed logins\fR
ten failures in a row from one network, the client network (an IPv4 /24, an IPv6
/64) (also from the password form and the TOTP form of a logged-in session):
that network is locked out of the account for 15 minutes, doubling for every
further failure, a day at most; the user still signs in from another network. A
new password from an administrator, or unlock, ends the locks. The locks are
kept in the memory of the service: a restart forgets them
.TP 8
\fB<id>: row level security refused what the API allowed (a bug)\fR
PostgreSQL: the database refused a statement the service had allowed; the answer
was 500. Report it with the id
.TP 8
\fB<id>: karidns\-zone apply failed (exit status N): ...\fR
a tool broke or is not the version the service expects
.TP 8
\fB<id>: karictl reload <zone> failed: ...\fR
SQLite3: the reload did not happen; the change is in the database
.SS What to watch
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGET /api/v1/health\fR (no login): 200, or 503 when the database does not
answer within 3 seconds. It is cheap and may be polled once a second.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBGET /api/v1/servers\fR (PostgreSQL): per DNS server the generation it applied
and the zones that failed to load.
.IP \(bu 2
Errors with code \fBinternal\fR (500) and the lines above; \fBbusy\fR (503)
answers mean the tools pool (\fBworkers\fR, 64 more may wait) or the database is
saturated.
.IP \(bu 2
The sessions table stays small by itself: the service deletes expired sessions
(and, with identity providers, the sign-ins that never came back) at the start
and every hour.
.IP \(bu 2
Lines \fBauth: oidc NAME: sign\-in refused from ADDRESS: REASON\fR are the
answer to "why can't she sign in?"; many of them from one address are somebody
trying; \fBoidc: provider NAME ... cannot be used yet\fR is the provider's
documents not coming (the egress, the issuer, the provider down).
.IP \(bu 2
Disk: the audit log (\fBkaridns_auth.audit_log\fR, or \fBkaridns_audit_log\fR
and \fBkaridns_auth_audit\fR on SQLite3) grows with every change and is never
pruned by the service. Prune it as the owner when your policy says so: \fBDELETE
FROM karidns_auth.audit_log WHERE at < now() \- interval '1 year'\fR.
.SH BACKUP, RESTORE, UPGRADE
\fBBack up\fR the zone data and the auth data together, and the \fBTOTP key
file\fR with them: without that key the TOTP secrets in the database cannot be
read and every TOTP user has to enrol again (below). PostgreSQL: \fBpg_dump\fR
of the database (the schema \fBkaridns_auth\fR is in it; \fB\-n karidns_auth \-n
public\fR for the parts). SQLite3: \fBsqlite3 zones.sqlite3 ".backup
/backup/zones.sqlite3"\fR and the same for \fBauth.sqlite3\fR (the online backup
of SQLite, not \fBcp\fR: the files are in WAL mode). Keep the backup as
protected as the live files: it holds the password hashes.
.PP
\fBRestore\fR the database, then the key file with its mode (0600, the service
user), then \fBservice karidns_webadmin configtest\fR. Sessions and tokens come
back with the database; a restored session older than its idle time is simply
dead.
.PP
\fBUpgrade.\fR \fBpkg add\fR the new \fBkaridns\-webadmin\fR package (the
program is replaced, the configuration and the key file stay). Apply the new SQL
files again (they only create what is missing and replace the functions:
PostgreSQL \fBkaridns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR, then the role scripts; SQLite3
\fBkaridns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR, \fBkaridns_audit.sqlite3.sql\fR, and
\fBkaridns_auth.sqlite3.sql\fR on the auth file), run \fBservice
karidns_webadmin configtest\fR \[u2014] it names a schema that is not at the
version the service wants \[u2014] and restart. Upgrade \fBkaridns\-zone\fR and
\fBkaricheck\fR with the same release: the service refuses a \fBkaridns\-zone\fR
that speaks another JSON schema.
.PP
\fBRotate the TOTP key.\fR There is no re-wrapping: a new key makes every stored
TOTP secret unreadable. Stop the service, remove \fBtotp.key\fR (a new one is
made at the next start), and switch TOTP off for every user as the database
owner (\fBUPDATE karidns_auth.credentials SET totp_enabled = false, totp_secret
= NULL;\fR, or the same on the \fBcredentials\fR table of the auth file); the
users enrol again, a global master at its next login.
.SS When someone is locked out
.TP 8
a user forgot the password
an administrator: \fBPUT /api/v1/principals/{id}/password\fR (this also ends the
lock and the user's sessions, and revokes its API tokens)
.TP 8
a user lost the TOTP device
an administrator: \fBPOST /api/v1/principals/{id}/credentials
{"action":"reset\-totp"}\fR (TOTP is off, the secret is gone, the user's
sessions end; a global master enrols again at the next login). Not for oneself:
ask another global master. Without an administrator who can log in, as the
database owner: \fBUPDATE karidns_auth.credentials SET totp_enabled = false,
totp_secret = NULL WHERE principal_id = <id>;\fR
.TP 8
an account is locked from a network (ten failed logins from it)
sign in from another network; or an administrator: \fBPOST
/api/v1/principals/{id}/credentials {"action":"unlock"}\fR (the counts and the
locks of every network go, the password stays; the user's page lists the locked
networks); or wait out the lock (15 minutes, twice as long for every further
failure, a day at most)
.TP 8
every administrator is locked out
\fBkaridns\-webadmin \-\-create\-admin another\-name\fR (PostgreSQL: with
\fB\-\-bootstrap\-conninfo\-file\fR) as above
.TP 8
a token leaked
\fBDELETE /api/v1/tokens/{id}\fR (the owner or an administrator); disabling a
user revokes its tokens and ends its sessions
.SH SECURITY
What the service does, in short (the details are in the API reference,
"Security"):
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHostile input.\fR Every request body has a fixed shape; unknown fields are
refused. Names are canonicalised and checked against the zone at a label
boundary before anything else. The text of a record is checked for what could
end its line or hide the rest of it, and \fBkaridns\-zone\fR checks it
again. Every SQL value is a bound parameter; every word given to a program was
checked first; no shell is involved. Limits: headers 16 KiB, body 1 MiB (zone
import 16 MiB), a change set of 1000 RRsets with 1000 records each, 256
connections, 30 seconds for a body (300 for an import).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSessions.\fR A 256-bit cookie (\fBHttpOnly\fR, \fBSameSite=Strict\fR,
\fBSecure\fR under https, \fBPath=/api/\fR); the database keeps only its
SHA-256. Idle time 30 minutes, 12 hours at most; a new session at the login and
after the TOTP step; a disabled user's sessions end. Cookie plus token in one
request is refused. CSRF: the \fBX\-KariDNS\-CSRF\fR token, the \fBOrigin\fR,
and \fBapplication/json\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPasswords\fR are scrypt hashes (128 MiB, two at a time); a wrong user and a
wrong password cost the same time and give the same answer; ten failures lock
the account for 15 minutes and longer. Asking for the password again (change it,
switch TOTP off) is limited like a login.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBRate limits\fR (in memory): 5 logins a minute per address and per user name,
20 requests a second and 2 writes a second per principal. A flood of requests
with bad credentials is limited per address but still costs a database lookup
each: put a rate limit in the proxy (the nginx example does).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIdentity providers\fR: an ID token is accepted only with a signature of the
provider's own key set (never \fBnone\fR, never a shared secret), for this
client, with the nonce of this sign-in, and for a user a global master linked to
that identity; no account is made by a sign-in. The state of a sign-in is single
use and tied to the browser that began it. The provider can sign in as the users
linked to it and do nothing else; its documents are read with size and time
limits, without redirects, and only from https names. The details are in the API
reference, "OpenID Connect".
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe web interface\fR: its files are read once at the start and served from
memory (no path to traverse, no directory to list, GET and HEAD only); the
page's policy allows no inline script or style, nothing from another origin, no
frame, and no HTML string (Trusted Types), and the page tells the person when a
proxy or an extension made the browser block something; every text that comes
from the service (zone data, names, comments, the rows of the audit log, error
texts) is drawn as text, with control, right-to-left and zero-width characters
made visible, and no address, attribute or style is ever built from it (a name
that is \fB.\fR or \fB..\fR is "not found": the browser would take it out of an
address and the request would reach another resource); the CSRF token is kept in
memory and the cookie is not readable by the page; the answers of the API carry
\fBCross\-Origin\-Resource\-Policy: same\-origin\fR like the files. The tests
scan the source (\fBui_scan.test.js\fR), run the screens against hostile data on
a fake DOM and in Firefox, and the review of milestone M6 is
\fBui_review.test.js\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSecrets\fR: see the table of files. The service never logs a secret and never
reads the key material of the DNSSEC keys.
.PP
What it does \fBnot\fR do, so that you can decide:
.IP \(bu 2
On \fBSQLite3\fR whoever can write the files bypasses every rule. On PostgreSQL
\fBkaridns.principal\fR is a setting any session of the API role can make: the
service is trusted to set it to the logged-in user; RLS catches mistakes of the
service, not a compromised service process.
.IP \(bu 2
Every logged-in user can \fBlist all users, groups and record policies\fR
(\fBGET /principals\fR, \fB/rr\-policies\fR: their names are needed to
grant). In a deployment with tenants that must not see each other's user names,
do not give them accounts, or put the API behind your own front end.
.IP \(bu 2
A \fBglobal master\fR can do everything the service can, and a stolen global
master session (with its TOTP step done) is that. Keep few of them; use domain
masters for day-to-day work.
.IP \(bu 2
A subdomain master or domain master can ask for a reload (\fBPOST .../reload\fR)
of a zone it has a grant on, up to 2 requests a second.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no per-request audit of reads; the audit log records changes.
.SH MEASUREMENTS
Measured on 2026-11-08 on the FreeBSD 15.1 build host (a jail with 4 vCPU on a
shared host; PostgreSQL 18.6 with \fBshared_buffers\fR 128 MB in a private
cluster; Node 24.22; the service, \fBkaridns\-zone\fR and the database on the
same host; one request at a time; the median of 3 to 5 requests; times are the
client's, including the HTTP round trip and, for a change set, both runs of
\fBkaridns\-zone\fR). They are what \fBtests/run_webadmin_write_test.sh\fR and
\fBtests/run_webadmin_write_pg_test.sh\fR print as \fB[COST]\fR lines
(\fBBIG=1000\fR or \fBBIG=100000\fR makes the big zone). The zone is half A
records and half TXT records, one RRset per record.
.PP
\fBA change set of one RRset\fR (a \fBPATCH .../rrsets\fR of one record: the
check run, the permission check, the commit run; the dry run is the check run
alone):
.PP
.TS
lbw(12) lbw(12) lbw(12) lbw(13) lbw(13)
lw(12) lw(12) lw(12) lw(13) lw(13).
\&Zone	T{
SQLite3 dry run
T}	T{
SQLite3 change set
T}	T{
PostgreSQL dry run
T}	T{
PostgreSQL change set
T}
_
\&15 records	\&12 ms	\&20 to 23 ms	\&30 ms	\&60 to 63 ms
T{
1,000 records
T}	\&15 ms	\&24 ms	\&35 ms	\&81 to 84 ms
T{
100,000 records
T}	T{
464 to 467 ms
T}	T{
576 to 579 ms
T}	\&546 to 550 ms	\&1.9 s
.TE
.PP
A subdomain master pays about the same (SQLite3: 581 ms at 100,000 records) or
less (PostgreSQL: 0.8 s; the commit run under row level security tests each row
against the policy, and a subdomain master's policy is cheaper to test than a
global master's name test, below). The cost is two runs of \fBkaridns\-zone\fR,
each of which reads the zone once, so it grows with the size of the zone, not
with the size of the change. The reload and the loader's work come on top. The
tools run \fBworkers\fR at a time; a burst beyond that waits, and beyond 64
waiting answers 503.
.PP
\fBReading RRsets\fR (\fBGET .../rrsets?limit=500\fR, the median of the first 20
pages of the 100,000-record zone, 500 RRsets a page):
.PP
.TS
lbw(30) lb lb
lw(30) l l.
\&Reader	\&SQLite3	\&PostgreSQL (row level security)
_
\&global master	\&40 ms	\&22 ms (0.6.0: 683 to 843 ms)
\&domain master	\&39 ms	\&10 ms (161 to 381 ms)
T{
subdomain master (half of the zone)
T}	\&38 ms	\&11 ms (103 to 333 ms)
.TE
.PP
Zone of 1,000 records, one page of 500: 5 to 7 ms (SQLite3), 8 to 17 ms
(PostgreSQL). A page reads the keys of the 501 RRsets after the cursor (sorted
by the index of names, the first 501 kept), so every page of a big zone costs
about the same, not more the deeper it is. That held only since 0.6.1; two
things made 0.6.0 slower: PostgreSQL compiled every page for about 140 ms (the
planner's cost estimate for the policy functions is in the millions, above
\fBjit_above_cost\fR, so JIT switched itself on for a query that runs in 4 ms;
the service now sets \fBjit = off\fR in every transaction), and the cursor was
tested as \fBname > X OR (name = X AND type > Y)\fR, which the planner could not
use as an index range, so it read the index from the first name of the table and
filtered (a page at the end of the zone cost 600 ms, a page at the start 4 ms;
the query now adds the redundant \fBname >= X\fR). In 0.6.1 the 20 pages walked
478 ms in all as a global master (17,600 ms in 0.6.0, the same walk on the same
host). On PostgreSQL the policy of a global master tests every row with the
function \fBname_in_zone\fR (the exact name test of A-23: a row of the zone's
\fBdomain_id\fR whose name is outside the zone stays invisible), which is why a
global master still reads slower than a domain master (22 against 10 ms a page),
whose rows are covered by a grant on the zone with no function call per row;
both are a few times what SQLite3 costs without any policy.
.PP
\fBReading for the web interface\fR (item F; the same host and method, measured
on 2026-11-09 by \fBtests/run_webadmin_write_test.sh\fR and
\fB..._write_pg_test.sh\fR with \fBBIG=100000 ZONES=10000\fR: a zone of 100,000
records and 10,000 more zones with no records; a global master unless said; the
median of 3; the PostgreSQL column of the RRset rows was measured again on
2026-11-11 with 0.6.1, the 0.6.0 figure in brackets; the list of zones was not
measured again):
.PP
.TS
lbw(33) lb lb
lw(33) l l.
\&Request	\&SQLite3	\&PostgreSQL
_
T{
\fBGET .../rrsets?limit=500\fR (the comparison)
T}	\&39 ms	\&19 ms (0.6.0: 191 ms)
T{
\&... with \fBrdata=1\fR (one run of \fBkaridns\-zone render\fR for the 500
RRsets)
T}	\&56 ms	\&38 ms (202 ms)
T{
\&... with \fBfrom=\fR the middle of the zone
T}	\&43 ms	\&20 ms (187 ms)
T{
\&... with \fBcontains=\fR a text no owner has (the whole zone scanned)
T}	\&33 ms	\&694 ms (742 ms)
T{
\&... with \fBcontains=\fR a text half of the zone has, the first page
T}	\&49 ms	\&376 ms (501 ms)
T{
\&... \fBcontains=\fR a text no owner has, as a domain master / a subdomain
master
T}	\&30 / 31 ms	\&71 / 98 ms (174 / 207 ms)
T{
\fBGET .../zones?limit=100\fR of 10,002 zones
T}	\&16 ms	\&1113 ms
T{
\&... with \fBcontains=\fR a text one zone has
T}	\&3 ms	\&362 ms
\&... with \fBprefix=\fR (the comparison)	\&3 ms	\&357 ms
T{
\&... with \fBcontains=\fR a text no zone has
T}	\&3 ms	\&32 ms
.TE
.PP
\fBrdata\fR costs about 20 to 35 microseconds a record (17 ms for a page of 500
records on SQLite3, 11 ms on PostgreSQL): each record is read by the loader's
reader inside a small zone of its own and written by the zone-file writer, twice
(the second time to prove that the text reads back as the same
record). \fBcontains\fR on RRsets is a scan of the zone's rows: on SQLite3 it
costs no more than a page, on PostgreSQL a global master's scan costs 3 to 4
pages (the policy of a global master tests every row, above) and a domain or
subdomain master's scan about what a page of its own costs; it is one request
for a search box, not something a page load makes. \fBfrom\fR is an index range
and costs a page. On the zone list the scan for \fBcontains\fR is as cheap as
the \fBprefix\fR comparison.
.PP
\fBStatistics matter.\fR A table that has just been filled (\fBkaridns\-zone
import\fR of a big zone) has no statistics for the planner until the analyze of
autovacuum has run (within about a minute of the load). An earlier version of
the page query joined the keys with the records, and without statistics
PostgreSQL planned a nested loop that tested the policy 500,000 times: \fB2.9
seconds\fR for one page of a zone of 1000 records, and 0.9 s (global master) for
a zone of 100,000. The query is two statements now (the keys, then the records
of those names): without statistics, 21 ms at 1000 records and 0.7 s at 100,000
(global master). After a big import you can run \fBANALYZE\fR on the PowerDNS
tables by hand; nothing breaks if you do not.
.PP
\fBMemory.\fR \fBkaridns\-zone apply \-\-dry\-run\fR (the check run) of a zone
of 1,000 records: 12 MB resident; of 100,000 records: 87 MB; \fBexport\fR of
100,000 records (2.3 MB of text): 128 MB. With \fBworkers\fR at 4 that is up to
4 of them at a time, and an export is limited to 256 MiB of output. The service
itself is about 100 MB resident when idle. A password hash at the default
\fBscrypt\-ln = 17\fR needs 128 MiB while it runs and takes 240 to 250 ms
(\fBscrypt\-ln = 14\fR: 30 ms; 19: 1 s); at most two run at a time, so a burst
of eight logins takes about a second and holds 256 MiB, and more than 64 waiting
answers 503.
.PP
\fBNot measured:\fR more than one request at a time (the pool and the rate
limits bound it; nothing was loaded to find the limit), several DNS hosts, a
database on another host, and the loader's reload of a big zone (that is the
loader's: \fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR, "Measurements").
